cartridge "BACKGAMMON/COMPUTE'S GAZETTE/1985"

' BACKGAMMON
' A ColecoVision game based on "Backgammon", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, November
' 1985 (Commodore 64 and VIC-20 BASIC, disk 1985-11; no author is named
' in the listings or on the disk's contents file). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules and the computer player are the
' listing's (orig/backgammon.bas, the same in gammon_vic.bas), the board
' is drawn for the ColecoVision: wooden bar and tray, red and cream
' points, stacked checkers with a count on the fourth, dice as sprites,
' checkers that glide from point to point, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: you (white) play the computer (black). You move from point
' 24 (top right) round to point 1 (bottom right) and bear off into the
' tray at the bottom right; the computer goes the other way and bears off
' at the top right. Each turn the side to play rolls two dice (doubles
' give four moves). A point holding two or more of the other side's
' checkers is closed; a single one (a blot) is hit and goes to the bar,
' from where it must come in before anything else moves. When all your
' checkers are home (points 1-6) you can bear them off. First to bear
' off all fifteen wins.
'
' From the listing: the start position, both sides' dice, doubles as
' four moves, entering from the bar first, hits, closed points (two or
' more), bearing off with the exact die once every checker is on points
' 1-6 (the computer: 19-24), or with a higher die when no checker is
' further back; a side that cannot move loses the rest of its turn; the
' choice of who goes first; the win at fifteen off ("I WIN." / "YOU
' WIN."). The computer player is the listing's, step for step (lines
' 400-820): every (point, die) is weighed 128 from the bar, 64 bearing
' off exactly, 32 blot onto blot, 16 onto its own point unless it breaks
' a pair, 8 a hit, 4 from points 1-18, 1 for any legal move, 2 more when
' another die could later cover the landing; the first highest wins
' (dice in order, points from 0 up); with nothing legal it may swap its
' smallest die above 1 for the exact bear-off of its rearmost checker.
' It keeps the listing's blind spots (it does not look ahead, and may
' leave blots).
'
' Liberties: a joystick cursor instead of typed letters (pick a checker
' that can move, then one of the places it can go; the game lists only
' moves the listing would accept). The listing let you type NO to give
' up the rest of a roll and left it to you to see that you could not
' move; here a turn passes by itself when you have no legal move left,
' and there is no voluntary pass. The listing bore off with the first
' die not smaller than the point even when an exact die came later in
' the roll, and then refused the move if checkers stood behind (line
' 950); here the exact die is used first, as line 930 meant. The
' computer's moves are shown as gliding checkers rather than "MOVE FROM
' E TO H" lines, and its turn goes on without a key press. Pip counts
' and the games won by each side (since power on) are shown. Board
' letters, DRAW and the play-again question are gone (the title comes
' back).
'
' Controls: either controller. The stick moves the cursor between the
' checkers that can move; the left button picks one, then the stick
' chooses where it goes and the left button moves it; the right button
' puts it back. On the title, up and down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off and
' the display off. Music first in the sound table, the title tune played
' once. `set sprite count` always given. Own xorshift random numbers.
' Nothing flashes.

